rsion 3.6 using MARC21slim2MODS3-6.xsl\n        (Revision 1.117 2017/02/14)","languageOfCataloging":{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"}}}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Breaking news ","subTitle":"the remaking of journalism and why it matters now "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Rusbridger, Alan","role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author"}}]},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"biography"},{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"},{"@authority":"Medical Subject Headings","#text":"Autobiography"},{"@authority":"Medical Subject Headings","#text":"Biography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies."},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Autobiographies"},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Autobiographies."},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Biographies."}],"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"stk"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"Scotland"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2018"},"issuance":"monographic"},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Edinburgh :"}},"agent":{"namePart":"Canongate Books Ltd,","role":{"roleTerm":"publisher"}},"dateIssued":"2018."}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"xxiv, 440 pages ; 24 cm"},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"How do we know any more what is true and what isn't? We are living through the greatest communication revolution since Gutenberg in which falsehood regularly seems to overwhelm truth. In Breaking News Alan Rusbridger offers an urgent and agenda-setting examination of the past, present and future of the press, and the forces menacing its freedom. The news media have been disrupted by huge and fast-moving changes. The growth of social media and with it the ability of billions of people to publish has created a vast amount of unreliable and false news which now competes with, and sometimes drowns, more established forms of journalism. The President of the United States regularly lies to the public and brands his critics 'fake'. Politicians openly rubbish the views of 'so-called experts'. Where can we look for reliable, verifiable sources of news and information? What does all this mean for democracy? And what will the future hold? Reflecting on his twenty years as editor of the Guardian; and his experience of breaking some of the most significant news stories of our time, including the Edward Snowden revelations, phone-hacking, WikiLeaks and the Keep it in the Ground campaign, Rusbridger answers these questions and offers a stirring defense of why quality journalism matters now more than ever."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Intro -- Title Page -- Copyright -- Dedication -- Contents -- Introduction -- 1. Not Bowling Alone -- 2. More Than a Business -- 3. The New World -- 4. Editor -- 5. Shedding Power -- 6. Guardian . . . Unlimited -- 7. The Conversation -- 8. Global -- 9. Format Wars -- 10. Dog, Meet Dog -- 11. The Future Is Mutual -- 12. The Money Question -- 13. Bee Information -- 14. Creaking at the Seams -- 15. Crash -- 16. Phone Hacking -- 17. Let Us Pay? -- 18. Open and Shut -- 19. The Gatekeepers -- 20. Members? -- 21. The Trophy Newspaper -- 22. Do You Love Your Country? -- 23. Whirlwinds of Change"},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Alan Rusbridger."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (pages 383-386) and index."}],"subject":[{"geog